#{[17592186088205 "The diagram gives center $(1,3)$ and radius $3$. Substitute into $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$ to obtain $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=9.$$"] [17592186088208 "The diagram gives center $(-2,5)$ and radius $7$. Therefore $$(x+2)^2+(y-5)^2=49.$$"] [17592186088269 "Substitute the indicated parameter values: $a=(-2)^2+(-2)=2$, $b=0^2-0=0$, and $c=3^2+3=12$. Thus $a+b+c=14$."] [17592186088272 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =9({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$"] [17592186088281 "Using $-1\\le\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta\\le1$, we obtain $-4\\le x\\le2$ and $0\\le y\\le4$."] [17592186088292 "The squared radius is the squared distance from $C(-1,2)$ to $X(2,3)$: $r^2=(2+1)^2+(3-2)^2=10$. Hence the circle is $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=10.$$"] [17592186088312 "Compare $(x+5)^2+(y+1)^2=4$ with $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$. The center is $(-5,-1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt4=2$."] [17592186088355 "Translate the standard radius-$2$ circle to center $(-1,2)$:\n$$x=2\\cos t-1,\\qquad y=2\\sin t+2.$$\nAt $t=0$ it starts at $(1,2)$ and moves counterclockwise."] [17592186088368 "The displayed arc lies in the third quadrant, from $(-4,0)$ to $(0,-4)$. In $x=4\\cos\\theta$, $y=4\\sin\\theta$, these endpoints occur at $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=3\\pi/2$, respectively. Use $\\pi\\le\\theta\\le3\\pi/2$."] [17592186092573 "For a parametric curve, $$L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\,dt.$$ Here $x'=4\\cos4t$ and $y'=-4\\sin t$. Therefore $$L=4\\int_0^2\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2t}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186092574 "Differentiate: $x'=2t$ and $y'=3t^2+2$. Thus $$(x')^2+(y')^2=4t^2+(3t^2+2)^2=9t^4+16t^2+4.$$ Therefore $$L=\\int_{-1}^1\\sqrt{9t^4+16t^2+4}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186092597 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2)$."] [17592186092703 "The radius of $A$ is $r=\\sqrt{10+10}=2\\sqrt5$. Thus $B$ has radius $2r=4\\sqrt5$ and angle $\\pi/3$, giving $$B=(4\\sqrt5\\cos(\\pi/3),4\\sqrt5\\sin(\\pi/3))=(2\\sqrt5,2\\sqrt{15}).$$"] [17592186092704 "The coordinates of $A$ give $\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt2/2$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\sqrt2/2$, so $\\theta=-\\pi/4$ modulo $2\\pi$. The angle of $B$ is $-\\pi/2$ modulo $2\\pi$, and its radius is $1$. Therefore $B=(0,-1)$."] [17592186092705 "The diagram puts $B$ counterclockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $\\pi/4+5\\pi/12=2\\pi/3$ and its radius is $8$. Therefore $$B=(8\\cos(2\\pi/3),8\\sin(2\\pi/3))=(-4,4\\sqrt3).$$"] [17592186092706 "The diagram puts $B$ clockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $210^\\circ-75^\\circ=135^\\circ$ and its radius is $6$. Thus $$B=(6\\cos135^\\circ,6\\sin135^\\circ)=(-3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2).$$"] [17592186092826 "Differentiate: $x'=2t+2$, $y'=2t^2-2$. Their squared sum is $$4(t+1)^2+4(t^2-1)^2=4(t^4-t^2+2t+2).$$ Hence $$L=2\\int_{-3}^2\\sqrt{t^4-t^2+2t+2}\\,dt.$$ The square root keeps the speed nonnegative on the entire interval, including where $t+1$ changes sign."] [17592186092856 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\pi/4$, both coordinates equal $4\\sqrt2/2=2\\sqrt2$. Thus $P=(2\\sqrt2,2\\sqrt2)$."] [17592186092857 "With $r=8$ and $\\theta=240^\\circ$, $$P=(8\\cos240^\\circ,8\\sin240^\\circ)=(-4,-4\\sqrt3).$$"] [17592186092858 "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52).$$"] [17592186096968 "Multiplying by $3$ preserves direction and triples length. Multiplying by $-1$ reverses direction and preserves length. The correct diagram therefore has a long vector pointing northeast labeled $3a$, and a vector one-third as long pointing southwest labeled $-a$."] [17592186096969 "Divide the vector by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\widehat a=\\frac{a}{|a|}=\\frac15a.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac15|a|=1$, and the positive scalar multiple has the same direction as $a$."] [17592186097030 "Factor the first vector: $4a-12b=4(a-3b)$. Since this common vector is nonzero, equality with $\\lambda(a-3b)$ implies $\\lambda=4$."] [17592186097031 "We have $a-12b=-2(-0.5a+6b)$. Since the vectors are nonzero, equality requires $\\lambda=-2$."] [17592186097032 "The vector $-\\tfrac12a$ has half the length of $a$ and the opposite direction. The original vector points northeast, so the correct diagram shows a vector half as long pointing southwest."] [17592186097042 "The horizontal and vertical vectors are perpendicular, so\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n$$\n\nDividing by this magnitude gives the required unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}=\\frac15(a+b).\n$$"] [17592186097043 "North-east and south-east directions are perpendicular. Hence\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{20^2+13^2}=\\sqrt{569}.\n$$\n\nThe unit vector in the direction of $a+b$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{\\sqrt{569}}.\n$$"] [17592186097045 "A unit vector in the direction of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ is obtained by dividing by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{|\\overrightarrow{AB}|}=\\frac17\\overrightarrow{AB}.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac17(7)=1$."] [17592186097161 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\langle4,\\frac32\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$"] [17592186097177 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. The vector moves four grid squares right and seven down, so\n\n$$\nc=\\left\\langle2,-\\frac72\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad |c|=\\sqrt{2^2+\\left(-\\frac72\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{65}}2.\n$$\n\nDividing by the magnitude gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{|c|}=\\frac{2c}{\\sqrt{65}}.\n$$"] [17592186101208 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186101209 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186101210 "From $x=t-2$ we have $t=x+2$. Substitution into $y=t^2$ gives $y=(x+2)^2$, with no restriction on real $x$."] [17592186105459 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$"] [17592186105488 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105489 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-7)+(-1)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105490 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{c}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105560 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\hspace{0.167em}038) \\\\  & \\approx 597.44,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186105561 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 432.022\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 177.265\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & \\approx 609\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$"] [17592186105562 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & \\approx 376\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186105590 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-23\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-23,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-23)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{554}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186105591 "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(-3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(-3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186105592 "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105596 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)+4(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))+2(0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-6+4-4 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV = |-6| = 6.\n$$"] [17592186105597 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(-1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-2(-2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)+(-2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-3k+6-3 \\\\  & =-3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ -3k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1$."] [17592186105598 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-1))-2((-4)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))-((-4)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-5-2+23 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|16|=16.\n$$"] [17592186105691 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)  \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\nt \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105692 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\sin   t \\\\ 9 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\cos   t \\\\ - 9 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\sin   t \\\\ 12 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+12\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{144{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+81{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106038 "The scalar projections are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|d|},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac{d\\cdot c}{|c|}.\n$$\n\nHere $c\\cdot d=-2+2+4=4$, while $|c|=|d|=3$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c+\\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac43+\\frac43=\\frac83.\n$$"] [17592186106041 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186106042 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106061 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106085 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=3, \\\\ b & =AC=7, \\\\ m∠A & ={128}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{128}^{∘}=0.788\\,010,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{128}^{∘} \\\\  & =10.5\\cdot (0.788\\hspace{0.167em}010) \\\\  & \\approx 8.27,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186106086 "The sides adjacent to the $20^\\circ$ angle are $5$ and $13$. The triangle area is $$\\frac12(5)(13)\\sin20^\\circ\\approx11.11565466.$$ Rounded to two decimal places, the area is $11.12$."] [17592186106087 "Draw diagonal $BD$. In $\\triangle ABD$, $$BD^2=10^2+6^2-2(10)(6)\\cos62^\\circ\\approx79.66341.$$ In $\\triangle BCD$, the cosine rule gives $BD^2=9^2+CD^2-18(CD)\\cos51.8^\\circ$. The convex configuration shown selects $CD\\approx11.009953$; the other root gives a concave configuration. Add the two triangle areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(10)(6)\\sin62^\\circ+\\tfrac12(9)(11.009953)\\sin51.8^\\circ\\approx65.42354.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $65$."] [17592186106088 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & \\approx 139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186106089 "The large triangle $ABD$ has area $\\tfrac12(12)(9)\\sin117^\\circ\\approx48.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$. The concave quadrilateral is the part remaining after removing $\\triangle BCD$, so $$[BCD]=[ABD]-[ABCD]\\approx48.11435-15=33.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the answer is $33\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$."] [17592186106094 "Coplanarity means the scalar triple product is zero. Since $b\\times c=\\langle-8,-1,10\\rangle$, we obtain $$a\\cdot(b\\times c)=-8k-1+10=9-8k=0.$$ Hence $k=\\tfrac98$."] [17592186106098 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}+1\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106110 "Use the scalar-projection formula:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=|c|\\cos\\theta=14\\cos60^\\circ=14\\left(\\frac12\\right)=7.\n$$"] [17592186106120 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-8\\mathbf{i}+10\\mathbf{j}-11\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-8,10,-11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{10}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{285}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{285}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186106121 "The area of the parallelogram is $|a\\times b|$. Compute\n\n$$\na\\times b=\n\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\-2&-3&-1\\\\1&2&1\\end{vmatrix}\n=-i+j-k.\n$$\n\nHence the area is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt3.\n$$"] [17592186106122 "Take two sides with common initial point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle2,1,2\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\langle0,1,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is $\\langle-1,-2,2\\rangle$, with magnitude $3$. A triangle has half the area of the parallelogram, so\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\frac12(3)=\\frac32.\n$$"] [17592186106123 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186106124 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106125 "Use $PQ$ as the base. The side vectors are\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle2,4,0\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{PR}=\\langle1,3,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe altitude from $R$ to the line $PQ$ is\n\n$$\nRH=\\frac{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}|}{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}|}\n=\\frac{|\\langle4,-2,2\\rangle|}{2\\sqrt5}\n=\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{2\\sqrt5}=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}5.\n$$"] [17592186106126 "The source asks for $\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)$. Compute\n$$\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c=\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle,$$\nthen $\\langle3,1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle=-66+8-6=-64$."] [17592186106127 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{q},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106128 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (3\\mathbf{b}\\times (-2\\mathbf{c})) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =24 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106129 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106130 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106131 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106213 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot x+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106214 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+1\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106310 "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product. Here $b\\times c=\\langle17,-10,-11\\rangle$, so $$V=|a\\cdot(b\\times c)|=|-3(17)-2(-11)|=|-29|=29.$$"] [17592186106347 "The area formula gives $$\\tfrac12(5)(6)\\sin A=\\tfrac{15\\sqrt3}{2}\\implies\\sin A=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$$ The marked angle is acute in the given diagram, selecting $A=60^\\circ$ rather than its obtuse supplement."] [17592186106439 "Split the convex quadrilateral along $BD$ into triangles with included angles $80^\\circ$ and $50^\\circ$. Add their areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(5)(8)\\sin80^\\circ+\\tfrac12(10)(7)\\sin50^\\circ\\approx46.50771.$$ Rounded to the nearest tenth, the area is $46.5$."] [17592186106478 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114970 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+2(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114971 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
